%N A401397 Triangle read by rows: T(n,k) is the number of decimal strings of length n with k indispensable digits, with 0 <= k <= n.
%C A401397 A digit is indispensable if it is greater than the first different digit to its right, with an imaginary 0 after the units digit (Choi, 2019, Def. 3.1).
%C A401397 A theorem of Choi (2019, Thm. 4.4) states that the digit sum of 9*m is 9 times the number of indispensable digits of m, so T(n,k) is the number of m with 0 <= m < 10^n such that the digit sum of 9*m equals 9*k.
%C A401397 Also, T(n,k) counts the m < 10^n for which the schoolbook subtraction 10*m - m has exactly k borrows, since the digit sum of 9*m = 10*m - m equals 9 times the number of borrows.
%C A401397 T(n,1) counts the decimal strings of length n whose digits, after trailing zeros are removed, never decrease from left to right, except that the final step must go strictly up; i.e., the last digit exceeds the preceding digit, with a missing preceding digit counted as 0.
%C A401397 T(n,n) counts the strings of length n with no zero digit and with digits that never increase from left to right.
%H A401397 J. Y. Choi, <a href="https://doi.org/10.7546/nntdm.2019.25.2.40-48">Indispensable digits for digit sums</a>, Notes Number Theory Discrete Math 25 (2019), pp. 40-48.
%H A401397 J. Y. Choi, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL22/Choi/choi15.html">Digit sums generalizing binomial coefficients</a>, J. Integer Seq. 22 (2019), Article 19.8.3.
%F A401397 T(n,k) = Sum_{i=0..n} (-1)^i*binomial(n,i)*(binomial(9*k-10*i+n, n) - binomial(9*k-9-10*i+n, n)), where binomial(m,n) = 0 for m < n, including m < 0.
%F A401397 T(n,k) = Sum_{i=0..8} A213651(n, 9*k-i), where A213651(n,s) = 0 for s < 0 (Choi 2019).
%F A401397 T(n,k) = A213651(n+1, 9*k+1) - A213651(n, 9*k+1) = A213651(n+1, 9*k) - A213651(n, 9*k-9).
%F A401397 T(n,0) = 1.
%F A401397 T(n,1) = binomial(n+9,9) - 1 = A035927(n) for n >= 1.
%F A401397 T(n,n) = binomial(n+8,8) = A000581(n+8).
%F A401397 Sum_{k=0..n} T(n,k) = 10^n = A011557(n).
%F A401397 Sum_{k=0..n} k*T(n,k) = 5*n*10^(n-1) + 4*(10^n-1)/9 for n >= 1.
%e A401397 Triangle begins:
%e A401397   n\k  0     1       2       3       4      5     6
%e A401397   0:   1;
%e A401397   1:   1,    9;
%e A401397   2:   1,   54,     45;
%e A401397   3:   1,  219,    615,    165;
%e A401397   4:   1,  714,   4620,   4170,    495;
%e A401397   5:   1, 2001,  25212,  50412,  21087,  1287;
%e A401397   6:   1, 5004, 111573, 411048, 381723, 87648, 3003;
%o A401397 (Python)
%o A401397 from collections import defaultdict
%o A401397 def row(n):
%o A401397     st = {(1, 0): 1}
%o A401397     for _ in range(n):
%o A401397         new = defaultdict(int)
%o A401397         for (t, j), c in st.items():
%o A401397             for d in range(10):
%o A401397                 if d >= t: new[(d, j+1)] += c
%o A401397                 else:      new[(d+1, j)] += c
%o A401397         st = new
%o A401397     out = [0]*(n+1)
%o A401397     for (t, j), c in st.items(): out[j] += c
%o A401397     return out
%o A401397 print([x for n in range(9) for x in row(n)])
